\f0\b\fs28 A Few Notes On Scaling\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \
- I don\'92t know if this will prove useful when you can simply use an amplifier or attenuator module and adjust to needs but I\'92m gonna dip a little into the math just for the sake of completeness and to provide inspiration - who knows where it might apply usefully\'85
\fs26 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \
Basic Scaling
\f1\b0\fs26 \
\
- Just to clarify, when talking about "scaling" here, what is meant is multiplying or dividing an analogue signal by a\'a0fixed\'a0value (known as the scaling coefficient).\
- So, doubling or halving the value of a signal are examples of scaling.\
- The key thing to note is that it is an operation that has a single input, so is very different to more familiar multiplication and division operations, where you you would want to multiply one analogue signal by another.\
- in fact it is the same thing occurring, just one signal in the equation comes from a fixed, internal [to the circuit] voltage source [the scaling coefficient].\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Basic Integer Up-Scaling
\f1\b0 \
- Scaling a signal up is actually quite a simple thing to do, as long as you only want to scale it by an integer (e.g. doubling, tripling, etc., not multiplying by 2.5, for example).\
For example, a coefficient of three (which it turns out is a very useful scaling coefficient, see later):\
\
z = kx\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 z = 3x\cb1 \
\cb3 z = x + x + x\cb1 \
\cb3 \
Where k is the scaling coefficient.\
\
- We simply add the signal to itself k times.\
- fine until k becomes unreasonably large, at which point it may be significantly more efficient to find the\'a0\cf8 prime factors*\cf2 \'a0of the number and re-express the addition using that.\
- For the example of k = 100:\
\
z = kx\cb1 \
\cb3 z = 100x\cb1 \
\cb3 z = (2 . 2 . 5 . 5) x\cb1 \
\cb3 \
- Rather than an adder with 100 stages, instead 2 two-stage adders and 2 5-stage adders can be used, which is significantly more efficient with respect to both resources and tedious \'91wiring\'92.\
\
\

\fs20 \cb5 \
* 
\f5 \cf10 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec10 every natural number greater than 1 is either a prime itself or can be\cf8 \'a0\'91\strokec11 factorized\'92\strokec10 \'a0\cf10 as a product of primes that is unique\cf8 \'a0\strokec11 up to\strokec10 \'a0\cf10 their order.\
The numbers greater than 1 that are not prime are called\cf0 \'a0\strokec11 composite numbers.\cf11 \super \
\cf10 \nosupersub \strokec10 Writing a number as a product of prime numbers is called a\'a0
\f6\i prime factorization
\f5\i0 \'a0of the number.\
The terms in the product are called\'a0
\f6\i prime factors
\f5\i0 . The same prime factor may occur more than once.\
Using this knowledge we can break scaling coefficients  down into manageable factors that can be manipulated with            smaller adder building blocks.\
\

\f1\fs26 \cf2 \cb3 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
